residents in the Pacific Northwest may  soon be paying a lot more in taxes  to fight a man-made climate change  process which many people say has never  been scientifically proven as you can  imagine some of those people are  fighting that plan correspondent Dan  Springer fills us in from Seattle  tonight when it comes to greenhouse  gases and their impact on the planet  President Trump has said only that he  will pull the u.s. out of the Paris  climate agreement which aims to reduce  emissions as a way to stop global  warming but West Coast states are  driving in the opposite direction with  efforts to tax carbon this is both an  economic benefit for the state of  Washington it is a job creator jobs in  the clean energy sector are growing  twice as fast as the rest of the economy  Washington state's Democratic governor  wants to tax residents every time they  fill up at the pump heat their homes or  plug in their electric vehicles  businesses like Boeing that compete  internationally would be exempt to hit  to residents and small businesses 3.3  billion dollars over four years the  money would go to green industries  you're not just subsidizing clean energy  or helped the environment you're also  subsidizing a big expansion in  government bureaucracy which does  nothing to help the environment British  Columbia was the first in north america  to tax carbon in 2008 the results mostly  good emissions are slightly down even as  the population and economy grow but they  also cut taxes to make up for higher  energy costs California already has a  cap-and-trade program while Oregon  lawmakers consider a similar plan it's  all an attempt to change behavior when  you have pollution going up into the  atmosphere for free that's a market  failure and a carbon tax corrects for  that market failure within the market  the fate of the carbon tax bills here in  the Pacific Northwest is uncertain  meantime a similar movement is forming  in the Northeast a bill introduced in  Massachusetts would also tax energy use  the residents there we get a rebate at  the end of the year  Bret Dan stringer in Seattle Dan thanks
 

